module gunbc.spark.rendezvous_participation

import std.types { String, Bool, List, NonEmptyStr, Int }
import std.nat { Nat }
import v2.std.algebra { length, filter }
import v2.std.optional { Present, Absent }
import product.placement_supply { HostIdentity }
import extdeps.iproute2.ss { ss_established_to_peer_argv, ss_enumeration_line_count }
import extdeps.systemd {
  SystemdUnitProperty, InvocationIDProperty, systemd_unit_property_wire,
}
import extdeps.systemd.unit_file {
  SystemdServiceMode, ActiveStateEstablishes, ExecStarted, ExecCompleted, ServiceAnnouncedReady,
  ReadinessFromActiveState, ReadinessEstablishedByService, ReadinessNotEstablished,
  systemd_readiness_from_active_state,
}
import gunbc.typed_argv_exec { typed_argv_exec_over_fleet_ssh, TypedArgvExecConverged, TypedArgvExecRefused }
import gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor { FleetSshExecutionContext, SshTarget }
import gunbc.fleet_ssh_locus { fleet_locus_ssh_target }

// WHETHER A DISTRIBUTED SERVING ARM IS PARTICIPATING, WHICH ITS SUPERVISOR CANNOT ANSWER.
//
// A four-rank tensor-parallel arm is one service whose liveness is a property of the GROUP: rank 0
// opens a torch TCPStore and the other three must attach to THAT store, not to some earlier one.
// Nothing in systemd can observe that. The ranks are declared Type=simple, so
// extdeps.systemd.unit_file systemd_active_state_establishes reduces their ActiveState=active to
// ExecStarted -- the manager forked a process -- and a process attached to a store that no longer
// exists satisfies it perfectly.
//
// THE FAILURE THIS MODULE EXISTS FOR WAS MEASURED, NOT ANTICIPATED. On 2026-09-16 the group-B rail
// dropped while its transceivers were reprogrammed. The head's unit restarted cleanly and opened a
// fresh store; the three worker ranks stayed in the store they had joined 46 minutes earlier, spinning
// on `Failed to check the "should dump" flag on TCPStore ... Broken pipe`. Every one of the four units
// reported ActiveState=active throughout. Restart=always never fired, because from systemd's vantage
// there was nothing wrong: four processes, four running services, zero participation. The arm could
// not answer a request and no unit-level reading could say so. Recovery required a converge, which
// is out-of-band actuation for an ordinary fault.
//
// THE OBSERVATION IS A CONNECTION TO THE CURRENT MASTER, and that choice is what makes a stale rank
// distinguishable. A rank's own logs cannot settle it -- the line naming the store is printed once, at
// join, and survives in the file long after the store is gone, so grepping it answers "did this rank
// ever join a store" rather than "is it in this one". A live TCP connection to the master endpoint
// cannot be stale by construction: when the head restarts, its listening socket is replaced and every
// connection to the old one is gone. So an established connection to master:port is a connection to the
// store the head is serving NOW rather than to an earlier one -- the staleness question is closed by
// construction -- and its absence on a host that should hold one is evidence that host's rank is adrift.
// What the reading cannot close is WHOSE connection it is; that is the next paragraph.
//
// WHAT THIS ESTABLISHES, AND THE NAME IT IS ALLOWED TO USE. `ss -H -t -n state established dst <peer>`
// enumerates the HOST's socket table. It carries no process attribution -- the invocation deliberately
// omits `-p`, which needs privilege this leg does not ask for -- so what a non-empty answer establishes
// is that SOME process on that host holds a live connection to the master endpoint. It is not
// established that the process is the declared rank's engine, that the engine is the one this arm
// launched, or that it is running the node rank this roster assigns it. A stale container, a foreign
// process, or a rank started with the wrong --node-rank all satisfy the same reading.
//
// THE RANK IN EVERY ARM BELOW IS THEREFORE THE DECLARED ONE, AND IT SAYS SO IN THE FIELD NAME. It is
// the position gunbc.spark.native_serving_realization's roster assigns the host -- what this repository
// INTENDS that host to be running -- joined to a host-level connectivity reading. An earlier revision
// named the positive arm `RankAtRendezvous` and carried the same number in a field called `rank`, which
// asserted an observation about a rank from a reading that had never seen one. That is the fabricated
// receipt DESIGN section 5 forbids: the number was not wrong, the CLAIM ABOUT WHERE IT CAME FROM was.
//
// SO IT IS CONSUMED AS A NEGATIVE GATE AND NEVER AS POSITIVE RANK PARTICIPATION. Its one production
// consumer (gunbc.spark.serving_offer spark_ranks_adrift_detail) stops the line on a demonstrably
// unconnected or unreadable host and otherwise lets the checks that DO establish something decide. That
// asymmetry is exactly what the reading supports: a host with no connection to the current master is
// evidence its rank is adrift, while a host with one is merely not evidence that it is.
//
// THE CLIMB, NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT TO A READER. Binding the socket to the exact declared
// unit/container/process needs two things this leg does not have: a privileged `ss -p` (or a
// /proc/<pid>/cgroup join) to attribute the socket to a pid, and the pid's container, which
// gunbc.spark.serving_rank_observe already reaches -- its ObservedRankIncarnation binds a container id
// AND the effective `--node-rank` off that container's own argv. When those two join, the positive arm
// becomes a real DeclaredRankParticipatesInCurrentEpoch and may be consumed as one. Until then it is
// what it is called.
//
// AND EVEN THE STRONGER READING WOULD NOT ESTABLISH HEALTH. A connected rank is attached to the current
// store; it is not thereby loaded or able to serve -- a rank can be connected and still be twenty
// minutes from a loaded checkpoint. Connectivity is necessary and not sufficient, which is why it sits
// BESIDE the endpoint readback rather than replacing it.

// THE EPOCH IS THE HEAD'S INVOCATION, because the head's unit start is what creates the store. Two
// ranks are in the same rendezvous exactly when they are attached to the store one invocation opened,
// so the invocation id is the epoch's identity rather than a label chosen for it. It is already read
// on the serving path (gunbc.spark.serving_incarnation_observe reads InvocationID off the head unit),
// so this is a join onto an existing observation and not a second acquisition.
//
// THE MASTER ENDPOINT IS CARRIED BESIDE IT because the ranks are told to join an address and port, and
// that is the peer the participation query filters on. Deriving it here from the head identity would
// duplicate gunbc.spark.native_serving_realization's launch argv, which is the authority for what the
// ranks were actually pointed at.
type RendezvousEpoch sole_constructor {
  head: HostIdentity
  head_invocation: NonEmptyStr
  master_endpoint: NonEmptyStr
}

// THE CONSTRUCTOR, because a sole_constructor type is not writable outside its module. That
// restriction is the right one here: an epoch minted from anything other than a head unit's
// invocation would be a second answer to "which start of the head is this", so every caller comes
// through one door and the door's parameters say what an epoch is made of.
fn rendezvous_epoch(head: HostIdentity, head_invocation: NonEmptyStr, master_endpoint: NonEmptyStr) -> RendezvousEpoch {
  RendezvousEpoch { head: head, head_invocation: head_invocation, master_endpoint: master_endpoint }
}

// THE THREE ARMS ARE THREE DIFFERENT FACTS AND THE MIDDLE ONE IS THE POINT. A host that holds a
// connection, one that demonstrably holds none, and one nobody could ask are three different repairs:
// nothing, restart the epoch, fix the observation path. Folding the third into the second would make
// an unreachable host look like a detached rank and send an operator to restart a healthy arm; folding
// it into the first would be the fail-open this whole module is about.
type DeclaredRankRendezvousReach
  = DeclaredRankHostConnected { host: HostIdentity, declared_rank: Nat, connections: Int }
  | DeclaredRankHostNotConnected { host: HostIdentity, declared_rank: Nat, peer: NonEmptyStr }
  | DeclaredRankHostReachUnread { host: HostIdentity, declared_rank: Nat, cause: String }

fn declared_rank_reach_host(p: DeclaredRankRendezvousReach) -> HostIdentity {
  match p {
    DeclaredRankHostConnected { host: h, declared_rank: _, connections: _ } => h
    DeclaredRankHostNotConnected { host: h, declared_rank: _, peer: _ } => h
    DeclaredRankHostReachUnread { host: h, declared_rank: _, cause: _ } => h
  }
}

fn declared_rank_host_is_connected(p: DeclaredRankRendezvousReach) -> Bool {
  match p {
    DeclaredRankHostConnected { host: _, declared_rank: _, connections: _ } => true
    DeclaredRankHostNotConnected { host: _, declared_rank: _, peer: _ } => false
    DeclaredRankHostReachUnread { host: _, declared_rank: _, cause: _ } => false
  }
}

fn declared_rank_reach_line(p: DeclaredRankRendezvousReach) -> String {
  match p {
    DeclaredRankHostConnected { host: h, declared_rank: r, connections: n } =>
      join([h as String, " declared rank ", to_string(r), ": host connected (", to_string(n), " connection(s))"], "")
    DeclaredRankHostNotConnected { host: h, declared_rank: r, peer: peer } =>
      join([h as String, " declared rank ", to_string(r), ": host has NO connection to ", peer as String], "")
    DeclaredRankHostReachUnread { host: h, declared_rank: r, cause: c } =>
      join([h as String, " declared rank ", to_string(r), ": host connectivity unread: ", c], "")
  }
}

// ONE RANK'S READING. The leg not running and the leg running to an empty answer are kept apart for
// the reason the engine scan beside it keeps them apart: a refused leg has no population to count,
// and counting it as zero would report a detached rank on an ssh failure.
fn observe_declared_rank_host_reach(
  target: SshTarget,
  context: FleetSshExecutionContext,
  host: HostIdentity,
  declared_rank: Nat,
  peer: NonEmptyStr,
) -> DeclaredRankRendezvousReach {
  match typed_argv_exec_over_fleet_ssh(target: target, context: context, argv: ss_established_to_peer_argv(peer: peer)) {
    TypedArgvExecRefused { reason: why } =>
      DeclaredRankHostReachUnread { host: host, declared_rank: declared_rank, cause: join(["the ss leg did not run: ", why], "") }
    TypedArgvExecConverged { result: r } =>
      if !r.success {
        DeclaredRankHostReachUnread { host: host, declared_rank: declared_rank, cause: join(["ss exited ", to_string(r.exit_code), ": ", r.stderr], "") }
      } else {
        let n = ss_enumeration_line_count(stdout: r.stdout)
        if n == 0 {
          DeclaredRankHostNotConnected { host: host, declared_rank: declared_rank, peer: peer }
        } else {
          DeclaredRankHostConnected { host: host, declared_rank: declared_rank, connections: n }
        }
      }
  }
}

// THE GROUP'S STANDING, which is the subject the serving path should have been asking about all along.
// `GroupHostsAllConnected` requires EVERY ranked host to be attached -- a partial answer is not a weaker
// yes, it is the exact state that produced the outage, so it gets its own arm carrying WHICH ranks are
// adrift rather than a count. A count cannot be acted on; a host can.
type ServingGroupRendezvousReach
  = GroupHostsAllConnected { epoch: RendezvousEpoch, ranks: List<DeclaredRankRendezvousReach> }
  | GroupHostsNotAllConnected { epoch: RendezvousEpoch, adrift: List<DeclaredRankRendezvousReach>, attached: Int, expected: Int }
  | GroupRendezvousReachUnobserved { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn serving_group_rendezvous_reach(
  context: FleetSshExecutionContext,
  epoch: RendezvousEpoch,
  ranked_hosts: List<HostIdentity>,
) -> ServingGroupRendezvousReach {
  if length(ranked_hosts) == 0 {
    GroupRendezvousReachUnobserved { cause: "the arm has no ranked hosts, so there is no rendezvous to observe" as NonEmptyStr }
  } else {
    let readings = declared_rank_reach_readings(context: context, epoch: epoch, hosts: ranked_hosts)
    let adrift = filter(readings, p => declared_rank_host_is_connected(p: p) == false)
    if length(adrift) == 0 {
      GroupHostsAllConnected { epoch: epoch, ranks: readings }
    } else {
      GroupHostsNotAllConnected {
        epoch: epoch,
        adrift: adrift,
        attached: length(filter(readings, p => declared_rank_host_is_connected(p: p))),
        expected: length(ranked_hosts),
      }
    }
  }
}

fn declared_rank_reach_readings(
  context: FleetSshExecutionContext,
  epoch: RendezvousEpoch,
  hosts: List<HostIdentity>,
) -> List<DeclaredRankRendezvousReach> {
  rank_readings_from(context: context, epoch: epoch, hosts: hosts, next: 0)
}

fn rank_readings_from(
  context: FleetSshExecutionContext,
  epoch: RendezvousEpoch,
  hosts: List<HostIdentity>,
  next: Nat,
) -> List<DeclaredRankRendezvousReach> {
  match hosts {
    Empty => []
    Cons { head: h, tail: t } =>
      concat(
        [observe_declared_rank_host_reach(
          target: fleet_locus_ssh_target(host: h),
          context: context,
          host: h,
          declared_rank: next,
          peer: epoch.master_endpoint,
        )],
        rank_readings_from(context: context, epoch: epoch, hosts: t, next: next + 1))
  }
}

fn serving_group_rendezvous_reach_line(p: ServingGroupRendezvousReach) -> String {
  match p {
    GroupHostsAllConnected { epoch: e, ranks: rs } =>
      join(["all ", to_string(length(rs)), " declared rank host(s) hold a connection to the store opened by invocation ", e.head_invocation as String, " at ", e.master_endpoint as String, " (host-level: the socket is not attributed to a process)"], "")
    GroupHostsNotAllConnected { epoch: e, adrift: ds, attached: a, expected: x } =>
      join([
        to_string(a), " of ", to_string(x), " declared rank host(s) hold a connection to the store opened by invocation ",
        e.head_invocation as String, " at ", e.master_endpoint as String,
        "; without one: ", join(map(ds, d => declared_rank_reach_line(p: d)), "; "),
      ], "")
    GroupRendezvousReachUnobserved { cause: c } => c as String
  }
}

// ================= WHAT THE SUPERVISOR'S OWN SIGNAL IS WORTH =================
//
// THE READINESS QUESTION IS ANSWERED BY THE UNIT'S TYPE, NOT BY THIS MODULE. A caller holding a rank's
// ActiveState wants to know whether it may conclude the rank is serving, and
// extdeps.systemd.unit_file already decides that from the declared mode. This wrapper exists so the
// serving path names the reason it needs an independent reading AT ALL, rather than the need being a
// fact a reader has to rediscover: a Type=simple unit's active state establishes ExecStarted, so
// something else must establish participation. This module supplies the host-level half of that
// something; the process-attributed half is the climb named at the top.
type ArmLivenessBasis
  = LivenessFromServiceAnnouncement
  | LivenessNeedsIndependentReading { because: NonEmptyStr }

fn arm_liveness_basis(mode: SystemdServiceMode) -> ArmLivenessBasis {
  match systemd_readiness_from_active_state(mode: mode) {
    ReadinessEstablishedByService => LivenessFromServiceAnnouncement
    ReadinessNotEstablished { mode: m, establishes: e } =>
      LivenessNeedsIndependentReading {
        because: join([
          "the unit declares Type=", m as String,
          ", whose ActiveState=active establishes ", active_state_establishes_wire(e: e) as String,
          " and not that the service is participating",
        ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
      }
  }
}

fn active_state_establishes_wire(e: ActiveStateEstablishes) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match e {
    ExecStarted => "that the manager started the exec" as NonEmptyStr
    ExecCompleted => "that the exec ran to completion" as NonEmptyStr
    ServiceAnnouncedReady => "that the service announced readiness" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// ================= HOW THE ARM RECOVERS, DECLARED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED =================
//
// AN ARM THAT CANNOT SELF-HEAL IS NOT A DEFECT UNTIL IT IS UNDECLARED. Group B's ranks hang instead of
// exiting, so Restart=always cannot rejoin them and only a converge restarts every rank into one
// epoch. That is a real posture with a real cost -- an ordinary rail blip needs out-of-band actuation,
// which DESIGN section 6 names as a tell -- and the answer is to SAY so, with the noticer named, rather
// than to leave the operator to discover it during an outage.
//
// THE THIRD ARM IS THE ONE THAT WAS TRUE ON 2026-09-16 AND IT IS DELIBERATELY UGLY TO WRITE. An arm
// that recovers only by converge, with nothing watching, is silently broken between the fault and
// whenever a person happens to look. Naming the noticer is the obligation the arm carries; leaving it
// unnamed is admissible only as ConvergeRecoveredUnnoticed, which exists so that state is
// representable and countable instead of ambient.
type ArmRecoveryPosture
  = SelfHealingOnFault { because: NonEmptyStr }
  | ConvergeRecovered { noticer: NonEmptyStr, converge_entry: NonEmptyStr }
  | ConvergeRecoveredUnnoticed { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

fn arm_recovery_is_noticed(posture: ArmRecoveryPosture) -> Bool {
  match posture {
    SelfHealingOnFault { because: _ } => true
    ConvergeRecovered { noticer: _, converge_entry: _ } => true
    ConvergeRecoveredUnnoticed { obligation: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE GROUP-B ARM'S POSTURE AS IT STANDS. The participation reading above is the noticer: a
// GroupHostsNotAllConnected is a typed, located refusal naming the hosts, which is what a converge decision
// needs. It is honest that the noticer only REPORTS -- nothing here restarts anything, because an
// automatic converge that bounced four engines on an observation would be an actuator built on one
// reading, and the reading landed tonight. The remaining climb is a rank that exits on rendezvous
// loss, which makes the posture SelfHealingOnFault and retires the converge dependency entirely.
data glm_native_arm_recovery_posture: ArmRecoveryPosture = ConvergeRecovered {
  noticer: "gunbc.spark.rendezvous_participation serving_group_rendezvous_reach, whose GroupHostsNotAllConnected arm names the hosts holding no connection to the current store. It notices the negative case only: a host WITH a connection is not thereby established to be running its declared rank, because the reading carries no process attribution" as NonEmptyStr,
  converge_entry: "gunbc.spark.native_serving_apply spark_native_serving_apply_wet, which restarts every rank into one epoch" as NonEmptyStr,
}
